WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.033
Go makes concurrent programming
approachable with goroutines, channels, context,

2
00:00:04.113 --> 00:00:05.536
and synchronization primitives.

3
00:00:05.616 --> 00:00:08.855
Those tools are powerful, but they still
need security-relevant discipline.

4
00:00:08.935 --> 00:00:11.536
Concurrency bugs often appear first as
reliability problems,

5
00:00:11.616 --> 00:00:14.413
yet they can become security problems when
they cause data mix-ups,

6
00:00:14.493 --> 00:00:16.848
stuck workers, unbounded resource use,
failed cleanup,

7
00:00:16.928 --> 00:00:18.744
or unpredictable authorization behavior.

8
00:00:18.824 --> 00:00:21.768
Context propagation is one of the most
important habits in Go services.

9
00:00:21.848 --> 00:00:24.939
A request context can carry cancellation
and deadlines through handlers,

10
00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:28.111
database calls, outbound HTTP calls,
queues, and background operations.

11
00:00:28.191 --> 00:00:31.430
When work continues after the caller is
gone, the service may waste resources,

12
00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:34.454
hold locks, or write results that no
longer belong to an active request.

13
00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:36.546
Long-running work should have clear
ownership.

14
00:00:36.626 --> 00:00:39.374
Goroutines and workers need shutdown
paths, cancellation rules,

15
00:00:39.454 --> 00:00:41.564
timeout behavior, and cleanup
responsibilities.

16
00:00:41.644 --> 00:00:44.932
Hidden background activity can make
incidents hard to understand and can leave

17
00:00:45.012 --> 00:00:46.582
resources open longer than expected.

18
00:00:46.662 --> 00:00:49.851
Files, network connections, database rows,
locks, temporary directories,

19
00:00:49.931 --> 00:00:52.532
and worker queues all need predictable
lifecycle management.

20
00:00:52.612 --> 00:00:54.624
Shared state should be protected
deliberately.

21
00:00:54.704 --> 00:00:58.090
Mutexes, channels, atomic values, and
immutable data patterns each have a place,

22
00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:01.163
but the choice should be documented enough
for reviewers to understand.

23
00:01:01.243 --> 00:01:04.384
Secure Go applications should fail
predictably under load, cancellation,

24
00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:05.347
and partial failure.

25
00:01:05.427 --> 00:01:07.979
Testing concurrency behavior is not only
about performance.

26
00:01:08.059 --> 00:01:11.543
It is about preserving trust decisions and
data boundaries when the service is under

27
00:01:11.623 --> 00:01:12.065
pressure.

28
00:01:12.145 --> 00:01:15.187
Resource limits belong in the design, not
only in production dashboards.

29
00:01:15.267 --> 00:01:18.309
Bound queue sizes, request deadlines,
upload processing, worker pools,

30
00:01:18.389 --> 00:01:21.334
and outbound calls according to the
service's real responsibilities.

31
00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:23.475
When work is bounded and cancellation is
honored,

32
00:01:23.555 --> 00:01:26.891
incidents are easier to contain and
security checks are less likely to be skipped

33
00:01:26.971 --> 00:01:28.100
under operational stress.
